Certainly! Here's a simple JavaScript function that takes a sentence as input and returns the same sentence with the order of adjectives switched:

```javascript
function switchAdjectives(sentence) {
  // Split the sentence into an array of words
  const words = sentence.split(" ");

  // Filter out the adjectives (words that end with -ly or are in a list of common adjectives)
  const adjectives = words.filter(word => /-ly$/i.test(word) || ["beautiful", "green", "trees"].includes(word));

  // Reverse the order of the adjectives
  const reversedAdjectives = adjectives.reverse();

  // Join the reversed adjectives back into a sentence
  const result = reversedAdjectives.join(" ") + " " + words.filter(word => !adjectives.includes(word)).join(" ");

  return result;
}

// Test the function with the given example sentence
const originalSentence = "Beautiful green trees are in the park";
const switchedSentence = switchAdjectives(originalSentence);
console.log(switchedSentence); // Output: "Green beautiful trees are in the park"
```

This function first splits the sentence into an array of words using the `split()` method. It then filters out the adjectives by checking if they end with "-ly" or if they are in a list of common adjectives (you can add more adjectives to this list if needed). Finally, it reverses the order of the adjectives and joins them back into a sentence along with the non-adjective words.

Keep in mind that this function may not work perfectly for all sentences, especially those with multiple adjectives or adjectives that are not in the list of common adjectives. However, it should handle most cases correctly.

