To call a PowerShell script from Terraform and allow for Read-Host input, you can use the `local-exec` provisioner in Terraform to execute the PowerShell script. Here's an example of how to do this:

1. Create a PowerShell script (e.g., `read_host.ps1`) that prompts the user for input using `Read-Host` and performs some action based on the input.
2. In your Terraform configuration file (e.g., `main.tf`), define a `local-exec` block to execute the PowerShell script. Here's an example:
```hcl
locals {
  my_variable = "Hello, World!"
}

resource "null_resource" "my_resource" {
  triggers = {
    always = "${timestamp()}"
  }

  provisioner "local-exec" {
    command = "powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File read_host.ps1"
    arguments = ["${local.my_variable}"]
  }
}
```
In this example, we're defining a `local-exec` block that executes the `read_host.ps1` PowerShell script with the argument `"Hello, World!"`. The `triggers` attribute ensures that the resource is re-created whenever the `timestamp()` function changes, which will cause the PowerShell script to be executed again with the latest value of `local.my_variable`.

When you apply this Terraform configuration, the `local-exec` block will execute the PowerShell script and prompt the user for input. The output of the script will be displayed in the Terraform output log.

Note that you may need to adjust the permissions and execution policy of the PowerShell script to ensure that it can be executed successfully. You can also pass additional arguments to the PowerShell script using the `arguments` attribute of the `local-exec` block.

